{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"STATUS UPDATE — `rebar doctor` now exists in core, with its first check. Filed from story affe-2b42-4ee4-4e12 (blocking-link comparability).\n\nMERGE PENDING: this epic and its counterpart both scope `rebar doctor` and overlap substantially. They are now linked `relates_to` so a brainstorm can reconcile them into one canonical epic. That merge is deliberately NOT done here — deciding which survives is the brainstorm's call, which is also why the link is `relates_to` and not `duplicates` (the latter would presuppose the outcome).\n\nWHAT SHIPPED (change 1040, stacked on 1039; both pending review at time of writing):\n\n`rebar doctor [--repair] [--dry-run] [--output json]` — read-only by default, exits 1 while any finding is outstanding so it can gate CI. It began life as a standalone `link-audit` verb; that was rejected for core, because rebar already carries three detect-and-remediate entry points (`fsck --repair`, `validate --fix`, and this) with two different flag names for \"fix it\". Renamed rather than adding a fourth verb.\n\nSole check today, named `dependency-graph`: net-active blocking links (`blocks` / `depends_on`) that disagree with the structural comparability rule shipped in 7ab3-9df0-7a90-4ffd. Three finding kinds — `ancestor-blocking` (a ticket blocking its own ancestor/descendant), `mis-escalated` (recorded endpoints differ from what the resolver returns), `unreadable` (an endpoint could not be reduced; reported, never repaired). Classification asks `resolve_hierarchy_link` rather than restating the rule, so the check cannot drift from the resolver it audits.\n\n`--repair` takes no store-wide lock (each write locks itself), refuses to run while a reconciler pass is in flight, and force-writes a `pre-doctor-repair` tag at the pre-run OID as a rollback anchor. It writes a replacement link BEFORE removing the stale one, so an interruption leaves a superset the next run converges rather than losing a dependency. Anything it cannot safely fix — a cyclic or closed-ticket replacement, or a pair whose pair-scoped `unlink` would cancel a different relation — is reported `unrepairable` with the original left intact, and the run continues.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT DO YET — i.e. what remains this epic's work:\n- No check registry. `doctor` hard-codes its one check; there is no `{name, category, run() -> {status, message, fix_command?}, is_required, auto_heal?}` contract, so a second check cannot be added without editing the command.\n- No crash isolation and no per-check timeouts. One check raising would abort the run.\n- No config, environment, or sync-artifact categories.\n- No composition of `validate` / `fsck` / `bridge-fsck`.\n- Flag vocabulary is still unreconciled across the three surfaces (`--repair` here and in `fsck`, `--fix` in `validate`).\n\nNEW SCOPE ITEM — re-parenting invalidates the dependency graph. Confirmed empirically, not theorised: the live tracker was remediated to zero scheduling-affecting findings, and within hours two NEW `mis-escalated` edges appeared, both with OPEN endpoints, so they genuinely distorted `ready`/`next-batch`. Comparability is structural, so changing a ticket's parent silently invalidates previously-correct blocking links without touching the links themselves — the store drifts on its own. Per operator direction this is in scope for this epic: re-parent/unparent should either auto-remediate the affected edges when the resolution is unambiguous, or be DENIED with a clear error when it would create an unresolvable situation. That makes `doctor` the backstop rather than the primary defence, and is the strongest argument for the auto-heal category this epic already describes.\n\nEvidence trail for the above lives on 9ad7-d5a2-a664-4827 (the check's implementation ticket): 18 legacy bad edges found on this repository's own tracker, 12 repaired, 8 residual and provably inert (every endpoint closed, so none can appear in ready/next-batch)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785410550979560001,"uuid":"54d1ea39-dc49-4d74-8b48-c6ba3f619fd3"}